`customFontColorMap` (and the analogous `customBackgroundColorMap`) uses `VennData['sets']` — a `string[]` — as the Map key. JavaScript `Map` compares keys with reference equality (`SameValueZero`), so two distinct array instances containing the same strings will never match. The lookups on lines 95 and 98–99 retrieve `(e as VennData).sets` from the D3 datum; this only succeeds if venn.js binds the exact same object references from the input `sets` array without copying them. If the library ever wraps or filters the data objects internally, custom `color` and `background` values on intersections will silently never be applied. The already-defined `stableSetsKey()` function should be used as the key instead:
```ts
const customFontColorMap = new Map<string, string>(
  sets.filter((s) => s.color).map((s) => [stableSetsKey([...s.sets].sort()), s.color!])
);
```
And the lookup should call `stableSetsKey([...(e as VennData).sets].sort())`.
